#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097833 :tutorial/content "A **unit vector** is a vector whose magnitude is equal to $1.$ \n\nGiven any vector $\\mathbf a$, we can create a unit vector $\\mathbf u$ by dividing $\\mathbf a$ by its magnitude $|\\, \\mathbf{a} \\,|,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{ \\mathbf{a} }{ |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| }\n$$\n\nFor example, if $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2,$ then the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\n![](images/c7/c78d5b75c1d5928e7b269a4a67229487b3d3324e9815b11c5fd1f47f63437d82.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a90fd4b3-d41f-589f-9067-39d57e3e8712" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2906 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105732 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105590} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f88dd688-449b-5a6d-8b91-78d00d0c932b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Triangle Spanned by Two Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105733 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105591} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c30217a-484c-5c31-a2bf-413f04e0edea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105592} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9331987e-8676-5bdb-b4ce-2129b1b09a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Altitude of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105916 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105488} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e598ad1e-cee5-5d5f-b2a9-c067e4625cf6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Scalar Projection Using Angles and Magnitudes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105489} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91691448-c59a-56dc-a775-d4e9c3297036" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Scalar Projection Using Components"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105490} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f" :knowledge-point/title "Projecting a Vector Onto a Perpendicular Vector"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106122}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106125}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106213}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107073 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $7,$ the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ has magnitude $6,$ and the angle between the vectors is $\\theta=45^\\circ.$ Then the magnitude of the cross product $\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =21\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,| \\sin\\theta$ is the formula for the area of the parallelogram with sides $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|$ and $|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|$ and the angle $\\theta$ between the sides. So the magnitude of the cross product represents the area of the parallelogram spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/27/2717ed1209f60dac12c3fcb07aea25f99c49b053fe0ce440b7ce996002a067c7.png)\n\n**Note:** Since in the formula for the area, we are interested only in the magnitude of the cross product, it does not matter whether we take $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\,|$ or $|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{a}\\,|$ as these vectors have the same magnitude (but opposite directions)." :tutorial/id #uuid "a31fffc9-7054-5bfa-af5f-2b515ddcb224" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2951 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107174 :tutorial/content "The **scalar projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the distance that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nTo illustrate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b},$ consider the point $P$ on the line that contains $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd37bdb2a97b4125fb360990d3c4a36b48bcb50433de033a154799db00e474dd.png)\n\nThe length $OP$ is called the **scalar projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or the **component** of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$). It is denoted as $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and we can compute it using cosine in $\\triangle OPA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =OP \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula is the general definition of the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0b270bce-4ef9-5021-bff5-83fdd5dd81e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9666 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
